  "answer_text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia or Ukraine depends on the distinction between practical control (*de facto*) and international law (*de jure*).\n\n### 1. In Practice (*De Facto*): Russia\nSince February and March of 2014, Crimea has been under **Russia's effective administration and military control**. \n* Following a military intervention by Russian forces, a highly disputed referendum was held in March 2014, which Russia used as the basis to formally annex the peninsula. \n* Russia administers the region as two federal subjects of the Russian Federation: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol.\n* Russian laws, currency (the ruble), taxation, time zone, and educational systems are fully integrated and active in the region.\n\n### 2. Under International Law (*De Jure*): Ukraine\nUnder international law and by the consensus of the vast majority of the global community, Crimea **belongs to Ukraine** and is recognized as temporarily occupied Ukrainian territory.\n* Immediately after the annexation, the United Nations General Assembly passed **Resolution 68/262**, which declared the Russian-sponsored referendum invalid and affirmed the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine within its internationally recognized borders.\n* The United States, the European Union, the United Kingdom, Canada, and nearly all other nations do not recognize Russia's sovereignty over Crimea. They view the annexation as an illegal violation of international law and the UN Charter.\n\n---\n\n### Key Historical & Geopolitical Context\n\n* **Soviet Era & 1991 Independence:** In 1954, Soviet leadership transferred Crimea from the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic to the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic. When the Soviet Union dissolved in 1991, Crimea remained an autonomous republic within the newly independent nation of Ukraine. In 1994, under the Budapest Memorandum, Russia formally committed to respecting Ukraine\u2019s independence and existing sovereign borders (which included Crimea) in exchange for Ukraine giving up its nuclear arsenal.\n* **The 2014 Annexation:** Following the 2014 Ukrainian Revolution (the Revolution of Dignity), which ousted pro-Russian President Viktor Yanukovych, Russian special forces without insignia occupied strategic sites in Crimea. Russia then orchestrated a swift referendum on joining Russia. The vote was widely criticized as illegal, coercive, and rushed, taking place under military occupation and without credible independent observers.\n* **The Ongoing Conflict:** Crimea has remained a central focus of the broader Russia-Ukraine war. Ukraine's official stance is that the war will not end until Crimea is fully liberated and returned to Ukrainian control. Ukrainian forces frequently target Russian military logistics, naval bases, air defenses, and supply routes (such as the Kerch Strait Bridge) on and around the peninsula.",
